    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Compartmentatilization of hydrocarbon reservoirs by faults is a widely observed phenomenon in the North Sea and the Niger della oil fields among others. Faults with significant throw or heave are identifiable in seismic surveys. However,the hydrologic properties of the faults cannot be predicted from the seismic data and toward the fault terminations or near fault-relay structures, slip decreases so that portions of the faults maybe invisible in seismic data.\r\n\r\nIn this study we use the Entrada sandstone, Arches National Park, Utah, as an outcrop analog of\r\na faulted sandstone reservoir. Field measurements of the geometry, width and permeability of normal faults in the Entrada Sandstone are represented in two-dimensional fluid flow models of fault-bounded sandstone reservoirs. Production from wells tapping these blocks is simulated with a high- resolution finite element code.\r\n\r\nFault structure and heterogeneous permeability, as mapped and measured, respectively, in the ArchesPark, lead to a fault signature in drawdown curves and derivative plots that differs significantly\r\nfrom that of idealized impermeable barriers. Fault segmentation or terminations may be detected in transient tests, if the general fault trend is seismically determined.\r\n\r\nHighly permeable slip planes facing the well create the illusion of a non-sealing or not-existing\r\nfault since remote formation waters are focused along these planes toward the well. Measured low\r\nfault-normal permeability may suffice to compartmentalize sandstone reservoirs; large pressure differentials can build up across faults during production.\r\n\r\nReservoir flow paths are complex. Inflow of extraneous formation water into the reservoir is likely to Occur along the permeable slip planes of the normal faults. Thus care should be taken for managing reservoirs with faults similar to those modeled here for not only decreased permeability perpendicular to the fault but also increased permeability parallel to the fault.",
